  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nA pattern transition begins Saturday as an upper level trough \nmoves across the Pacific Northwest and pushes a dry cold front \nthrough southeast Oregon and southwest Idaho Saturday afternoon \ninto Sunday morning. Minimal atmospheric moisture will limit \nprecipitation potential with this passage, keeping conditions \npredominantly dry across the area.\n\nThe primary impact from this frontal passage will be strong and\ngusty winds. West to northwest surface winds will increase to \n20 to 30 mph with gusts up to 40 mph along and behind the front \nSaturday afternoon and evening. Combined with low relative \nhumidity, these gusty winds will result in heightened fire \nweather concerns across the region. This wind threat will \ncontinue into Sunday across southwest Idaho with the tight \nsurface pressure gradient behind the exiting front.\n\nBehind the front, daytime temperatures will drop 5 to 10 degrees\non Sunday and Monday, bringing high temperatures back down to\nnear seasonal normals. High pressure will begin to rebuild over\nthe Great Basin Tuesday into Wednesday, leading to lighter winds\nand a gradual warming trend through mid-week.",
